#show: docs-subchapter.with(
  title: [Existence of Atoms and Atomic Structure],
  route: "existence",
)

From Dalton's Law of Definite Proportions and the Greek's cutting argument, it becomes rather convincing that there is a small unit of matter. Given a microscope, Brownian motion further verifies the existence of such smallest unit of matter, define it as an _atom_.

Via radioactive material or Hydrogen gas in plasma phase, subatomic particles can be found to have charge and their charge-to-mass ratio may be determined through mass spectrometry. The Oil Drop Experiment @wikipedia_oil_drop_experiment_2026 gives the charge. Hence, the mass is known. Rutherford's experiment of bombarding gold foil with $alpha$ particles from $"RaBr"_2$ then shows the existence of positively charged nuclei and its size relative to the atom. The size of an atom may be found through X-ray Crystallography, by diffraction to get the distance between two nuclei. The charge-to-mass ratio of an electron (or $beta$ particle) then reveals the mass of the electron.

However, Newtonian mechanics would give the speed of the electron to be faster than light, which is impossible from Maxwell's equations. The break-through point lies in the ultraviolet catastrophe motivates the postulate of quantized energy, which gives rise to planck's constant. The same constant is then observed in the photoelectric effect as #lbl(<eqn:planck-energy>, $ E = h nu, $) and is verified by the fact that the excitation of the electrons dependent on the frequency but not intensity of the incident light. This assumption gives the particle-like description of light, the _photon_; however, Thomas Young showed in the Double Slit Experiment that light is also a particle. The particle light duality of light and the equation for its momentum $p = E/lambda$ motivates the wave description for matter.
